An `&>` that hugs the word before it. The shell reads `cmd&>/dev/null` as a
redirection of both streams -- there is no ambiguity in it -- and v0 refuses
it anyway, because a redirection here must stand as its own word. What the
refusal buys is the neighbouring case: `cmd& >/dev/null` is a background
marker and a redirection of the NEXT command, and the two lines differ by one
space. `background` carries the `!">"` lookahead so this one is refused rather
than read as the other.
